Z||000000||85ZainoGT||11-03-2001||11:25 PM||||Anyone have it, I am buying an 87gt auto soon, just wonderin what everyone though about it?<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||reddog||11-03-2001||11:58 PM||||I was interested in it too. Haven't heard any comments (good or bad) on it. I just bought a 87GT 5speed to fix up. Will be switching the auto out of my Yellow 86 to the 5speed. Put a shift kit on my Kawasaki Ninja ZX6R it worked wonders don't know if it is the same thing though!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sammygif.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||11-04-2001||10:20 AM||||Isn't that the controversial copy of Rodney Dickman's kit? If it is, the Fiero store gave them all to Rodney (by their letter to him). If not ignore this.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-04-2001||11:34 AM||||The shift kit is a Trans-Go. I have one and it works great. It's more of a "heavy duty" kit than a performance kit. Shifting is way better and it prevents bleed down of the torque converter during "spirited" driving.<BR>This will explain the hows and whys of the kit: <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||11-04-2001||12:15 PM||||Definitely some nice, hard shifts in Oreifs car!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'd buy it if I still had an auto.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SKIDMARK||11-04-2001||12:25 PM||||I just installed a Fiero Store shift kit in my transaxle. Unfortunately, I won't be able to drive the car for a couple of months due to refurbishing the entire car. I can tell you that it was simple to install on the bench and I understand that you can actually install it with the trans in the car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've heard tire chirping into second and third gear stories and things like that but can't say first hand how true they are. I will let you all know in a couple of months how it all turns out. The 3.4 swap I'm doing should make some difference too!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||6
Z||000006||FieroRacer||11-04-2001||08:09 PM||||I have one waiting to go into Demon at the same time the engine repairs and new tires are put on.  Hopefully December will be my month.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||11-05-2001||12:03 AM||||Oreif: If you don't mind me asking, how much did your shift kit run you?  You can say "less than $100" if you want.  Does it make a difference in performance?  Can you chirp yours into second?  Have you tried manually shifting?  Does it do any better?  Sorry for the inquizition.  Just wondering.  Thanks for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||11-05-2001||03:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>Oreif: If you don't mind me asking, how much did your shift kit run you?  You can say "less than $100" if you want.  Does it make a difference in performance?  Can you chirp yours into second?  Have you tried manually shifting?  Does it do any better?  Sorry for the inquizition.  Just wondering.  Thanks for any help.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I originally bought mine from IRM. The Fiero Store is selling the same kit. I paid $39.95.<BR>When I first put it in I was able to "chirp" the tires when it shifted from first to second with the car floored. (I had "cheap" tires on the car) With the new tires and suspension changes I have now, it lurches the car forward. (Tires stick real good!) For the low cost, I feel it is highly worth it. As for 0-60 times it probably knocked a few 10th's off. Last time I checked my 0-60 with a G-Tech it was 6.9 seconds. I haven't had a chance to try it out this year with the new exhaust and bored throttle body.<P>As for installing it, I had a trans shop install it. They had a $69.95 trans flush and fill special and they installed it at no additional cost. (had a friend who worked there)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||11-05-2001||01:32 PM||||shift kit on a ZX ? uuum.. CO2 i hope... or just a steel/friction change? anywho... <BR> <A HREF="http://www.northernautoparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.northernautoparts.com</A>  they have a cheaper and just as effective shift kit for our cars.. i've got one. used to chirp 2nd, 3rd.. i changed the plate to the towing one and now i can drive it like a normal car AND beat on it.  <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91<P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...||||reg||1
Z||000010||grinthock||11-05-2001||06:45 PM||||I was told by the FieroShop / RaceTech that if you put in a shift kit you should put on a tranny cooler...  <P>I did, but have yet to do the shiftkit, I will do it when I change my transmission fluids||||reg||1
Z||000011||fropuff||11-05-2001||06:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR>...used to chirp 2nd, 3rd.. i changed the plate to the towing one and now i can drive it like a normal car AND beat on it.  <BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1FST2M6: Was this before you got nitrous?  I thought chirping in 2nd is good enough for the 125.  Do you have any part numbers?  How about 0-60?  Did it decrease it very much?  Thanks for any help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||grinthock||11-05-2001||06:59 PM||||That's the other thing that drives me nuts!  1FST2M6 is running AMAZING times, ON AN AUTO!  Makes me wanna keep my auto more and more and more!<P>Has anyone here installed one yourself?  I mean I have jacks that will take the car a foot off the ground if I wanted....  I also need to do a tranny fluid change, never done that either.||||reg||1
Z||000013||grinthock||11-05-2001||07:03 PM||||What kits are people using here?  And where did you get them?  <P>Thx.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fropuff||11-05-2001||09:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B><BR>...I also need to do a tranny fluid change, never done that either.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A filter and gasket change is fairly easy.  I haven't taken a look underneath a Fiero, so I don't know how hard it is.  Mostly aren't hard though.  The b*tch is when you get ATF dripping down your arms and in your hair.  The only thing you have to watch out for is so you don't overtighten the bolts.  That's a b*tch too.  BTW: The shift kit can be found from the Fiero Store for around $35.  At least that's what it was when I last checked.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||grinthock||11-05-2001||11:40 PM||||The fierostore one isn't a bad deal either $35 includes a filter and gasget for the fluid change...  I just do'nt know how to chang ethe fluid and havn't found the instructions yet (my haynes has gone MIA!)||||reg||1
Z||000016||fropuff||11-06-2001||12:01 AM||||All you have to do is: pull the bolts out from around the tranny pan; keep two in on one side so it can drop down a little.  Let it drain a little, then take the other two out.  Be careful so you don't dump it all over yourself.  Okay, pull the gasket up, clean it up, and put another one one.  Pull the filter out, put the new one on.  Put the pan back on; make sure the gasket doesn't slide.  Put the bolts in; tighten them in a criss-cross pattern; don't over-tighten!  Okay, then pull the trans fluid dipstick out, then dump ATF down through it.  I usually start with 3-4 quarts; turn the car on, put it in drive (with foot on brake), let it engage, then put it back into park.  Do that a few times.  Then check the level.  Add accordingly.  That's about it.  And check to be sure you tightened the pan good enough.  <P>BTW: If I am forgetting anything, please add.  I don't want to be the person that's responsible for a f'd up tranny.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||11-06-2001||01:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>All you have to do is: pull the bolts out from around the tranny pan; keep two in on one side so it can drop down a little.  Let it drain a little, then take the other two out.  Be careful so you don't dump it all over yourself.  Okay, pull the gasket up, clean it up, and put another one one.  Pull the filter out, put the new one on.  Put the pan back on; make sure the gasket doesn't slide.  Put the bolts in; tighten them in a criss-cross pattern; don't over-tighten!  Okay, then pull the trans fluid dipstick out, then dump ATF down through it.  I usually start with 3-4 quarts; turn the car on, put it in drive (with foot on brake), let it engage, then put it back into park.  Do that a few times.  Then check the level.  Add accordingly.  That's about it.  And check to be sure you tightened the pan good enough.  <P>BTW: If I am forgetting anything, please add.  I don't want to be the person that's responsible for a f'd up tranny.  Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>To add in the shift kit, Just follow the directions that come with it. You will take out the valve body inside and replace a couple of springs and ball bearings. Instructions are easy to follow. The only reason I went to a trans shop was so they could check out the rest of the trans and do a full flush.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fie Ro||11-06-2001||05:42 AM||||[quote] I was told by the FieroShop / RaceTech that if you put in a shift kit you should put on a tranny cooler... [quote]<P>shouldn't be necessary. with a shiftkit the tranny shifts faster and with less slipping/friction between shifts there's less heat building up so the kit itself should make it already run cooler!<P>Overheating is the main killer of automatics though.... in trucks<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||10
Z||000019||reddog||11-06-2001||08:06 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>shift kit on a ZX ? uuum.. CO2 i hope... or just a steel/friction change? anywho... <BR>  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah it's called a Ninja ZX6R(MOTORCYCLE).And the shift kit includes a stiffer spring for the shift mechanisim so that your quick shifts are more positive and more stiff. It eliminates skipped shifts and stripping out second and third gears. Hope that clears it up for you. <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||reddog||11-06-2001||08:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>shift kit on a ZX ? uuum.. CO2 i hope... or just a steel/friction change? anywho... <BR>  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah it's called a Ninja ZX6R(MOTORCYCLE).And the shift kit includes a stiffer spring for the shift mechanisim so that your quick shifts are more positive and more stiff. It eliminates skipped shifts and stripping out second and third gears. Hope that clears it up for you. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sammygif.gif"> ||||reg||1
